\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.0.1}{\ignorespaces The running tree \(T\) (\(n=15\)) and its centroid \(4\) (red). Cutting at \(4\) -- the dashed cuts -- splits \(T\) into the three pieces of sizes \(4,4,6\); each of size \(\le \lfloor 15/2\rfloor \). Those three pieces are recursed into independently in \(\S  \ref {sec:c18-tree}\), producing the centroid tree. Left piece \(A=\{1,5,6,11\}\), middle \(B=\{2,7,8,12\}\), right \(C=\{3,9,10,13,14,15\}\).}}{1464}{figure.caption.259}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c18-tree}{{24.0.1}{1464}{The running tree \(T\) (\(n=15\)) and its centroid \(4\) (red). Cutting at \(4\) -- the dashed cuts -- splits \(T\) into the three pieces of sizes \(4,4,6\); each of size \(\le \lfloor 15/2\rfloor \). Those three pieces are recursed into independently in \(\secref {c18-tree}\), producing the centroid tree. Left piece \(A=\{1,5,6,11\}\), middle \(B=\{2,7,8,12\}\), right \(C=\{3,9,10,13,14,15\}\)}{figure.caption.259}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.0.2}{\ignorespaces The chapter's five-state taxonomy. \emph  {cut} is the decomposition itself; \emph  {claim} and \emph  {index} are the two ways it pays off (count paths by their top centroid; index a component by its centroid distance table); \emph  {merge} is the general divide-and-conquer-on-trees template with a data structure in the bucket; \emph  {pick} is the boundary choosing between centroid and its neighbours. Each solved problem sits on one row: CF~321C on \emph  {cut}, CF~715C on \emph  {claim}, CF~293E on \emph  {index}, CF~833D on \emph  {merge}, the Summit CF~757G on \emph  {index}+\emph  {merge}.}}{1465}{figure.caption.260}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c18-taxonomy}{{24.0.2}{1465}{The chapter's five-state taxonomy. \emph {cut} is the decomposition itself; \emph {claim} and \emph {index} are the two ways it pays off (count paths by their top centroid; index a component by its centroid distance table); \emph {merge} is the general divide-and-conquer-on-trees template with a data structure in the bucket; \emph {pick} is the boundary choosing between centroid and its neighbours. Each solved problem sits on one row: CF~321C on \emph {cut}, CF~715C on \emph {claim}, CF~293E on \emph {index}, CF~833D on \emph {merge}, the Summit CF~757G on \emph {index}+\emph {merge}}{figure.caption.260}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {24.1}Recursion at the Balance}{1467}{section.24.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:c18-tree}{{24.1}{1467}{Recursion at the Balance}{section.24.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {24.1.1}The decomposition, and the tree it leaves behind}{1468}{subsection.24.1.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{def:c18-decomp}{{24.1.1}{1468}{Centroid decomposition and the centroid tree}{tcb@cnt@definition.24.1.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.1.1}{\ignorespaces The \textbf  {\textcolor {deepblue}{centroid tree}} of \(T\), built by \(\S  \ref {sec:c18-tree}\)'s recursion. Its edges are \(\mathit  {par}_c\) relations, not \(T\)'s edges: the top centroid \(4\) (red) roots the tree, \(1,2,3\) are its three piece-centroids, and the remaining nodes fill depths 2 and 3. The height of \(3\) obeys \(Theorem~\ref {thm:c18-height}\); CF~321C prints exactly this tree, reading each rank as \(\text  {'A'}+\mathit  {cd}\).}}{1469}{figure.caption.261}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c18-centtree}{{24.1.1}{1469}{The \term {centroid tree} of \(T\), built by \(\secref {c18-tree}\)'s recursion. Its edges are \(\mathit {par}_c\) relations, not \(T\)'s edges: the top centroid \(4\) (red) roots the tree, \(1,2,3\) are its three piece-centroids, and the remaining nodes fill depths 2 and 3. The height of \(3\) obeys \(\thmref {c18-height}\); CF~321C prints exactly this tree, reading each rank as \(\text {'A'}+\mathit {cd}\)}{figure.caption.261}{}}
